{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2956","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2956","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2956\",\"parent_revision\":\"d90f7426268b1a8929f97d37db70cb8d0911680d\",\"root_revision\":\"f7b4b2681274537ff27bf5db55f674639a480a5b59e8c066562be1358d6d0258\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-08-18\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nEvery `backlog_groom` call made during a grooming pass on #2953 and #2955 (16+ calls across 6 different agents plus the orchestrator, spanning two separate GitHub-backend items) returned `\\\"groomed_updated\\\": true` with zero errors, but none of the written content was retrievable afterward through `backlog_view` \u2014 via CLI or MCP, `summary=true` or `summary=false`. `sections_index` continued showing only the item's original 4 sections. Cross-checked directly against the live GitHub issue (`gh api repos/.../issues/{N}`): `updated_at` predated the entire grooming session, confirming nothing reached GitHub either.\\n\\nThe content was not lost \u2014 it exists in the local cache file (`~/.dh/projects/{slug}/github-cache/items/issues/{N}.yaml`), but under roughly 3x the expected number of top-level section keys. Two distinct patterns observed in the cache:\\n1. Duplication \u2014 a section written under its correct name (e.g. `Files`, `Resources`, `Impact`, `Impact Radius`, `Dependencies`, `Priority`, `Benefits`, `Research`, `Design Intent Alignment`, `Acceptance Criteria`, `Expected Behavior`) also appears a second time under an `unknown__{slug}` key with the same content.\\n2. Case/format mangling with no canonical counterpart at all \u2014 `RT-ICA` was stored only as `rt_ica`, `Fact-Check` only as `fact_check`, `Issue Classification` only as `issue_classification`; none exist under their documented Title-Case/hyphenated names anywhere in the file.\\n3. Per-claim fact-checker writes fragmented into separate top-level sections instead of one `Fact-Check` section (`unknown__claim_1:_\\\"...\\\"`, `unknown__claim_3:_\\\"...\\\"`, etc., with the full claim text embedded in the YAML key itself).\\n4. Even sections nobody touched this session (`Story`, `Context`) acquired `unknown__` duplicates.\\n\\nA follow-up investigation (2026-08-18) traced this to `_normalize_section_key()` in `backlog_core/operations.py` (canonical `SECTION_HEADING` map covers only 3 entries: `fact_check`, `rt_ica`, `issue_classification`; any other section name passes through unchanged on write) combined with `parse_issue_body()` in `backlog_core/github_sync.py` (any heading not in that same 3-entry map gets bucketed as `unknown__{slug}` on re-parse instead of matched back to the key it was just written under) \u2014 plus a separate, not-yet-fully-diagnosed failure in the GitHub push/replay path that means pending mutations never actually reach the live issue. Full diagnosis with file:line citations: `/private/tmp/claude-503/-Users-jamienelson-repos-claude-skills/2da56558-aee9-4992-9946-ab27b7af9cbb/scratchpad/reports/20260818-backlog-groom-visibility-bug.md`.\\n\\nImpact: any item groomed through the GitHub backend via `backlog_groom` with non-canonical section names \u2014 i.e. essentially every real grooming pass \u2014 silently produces an unreadable, duplicated section structure that never reaches the actual GitHub issue, while every tool call in the chain reports success. `mark_groomed=True` would advance the item's status label while its real content stays effectively empty to any consumer. This has been running unnoticed across many prior grooming sessions.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog_groom writes report success but are invisible via backlog_view \u2014 section-key duplication in _normalize_section_key plus broken github sync replay** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 discovered live while grooming #2953 via /dh:work-backlog-item, confirmed via root-cause investigation 2026-08-18\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-18\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Blast Radius Cleanup 2026 08 20\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPer user request, went further than the fix itself and cleaned up every item already corrupted by this bug, plus closed a third gap found during that cleanup. Commit `7922c2f4` (write-side guard), plus direct data-repair against the live GitHub backend for the affected items (not a code change \u2014 a one-time migration).\\n\\n### Additional fix: `_normalize_section_key` now rejects `section=\\\"Description\\\"`\\n\\nFound live on `#2979`/`#2984`/`#2985` while scanning the local cache for remaining corruption: a `backlog_groom(section=\\\"Description\\\", ...)` write produces a permanent, unrenderable orphan key \u2014 `github_sync.parse_issue_body` special-cases `## Description` as `BacklogItem.description`, never as a section, so no registry entry can ever make it resolvable. Same failure shape as root cause B (double-rendered heading, last-heading-wins data loss on reparse) but for a name a registry fix can't close. `_normalize_section_key` now raises a clear `BacklogError` for this name, pointing the caller at `update_item(description=...)` instead.\\n\\n### Every item corrupted by this bug (found via full local-cache scan) is now clean\\n\\n| Item | Corruption | Resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `#2953` | 13 bare duplicate section keys (`Priority`, `Impact Radius`, `Design Intent Alignment`, etc.) | Self-healed via `normalize` + targeted `reconcile` \u2014 fresh push, zero duplicate keys remain |\\n| `#2955` | `Working Register`/`working_register` duplicate | Already healed during fix validation (see above) |\\n| `#2975` | `Impact Radius`/`Resources` duplicates | Same normalize+reconcile pass as `#2953` |\\n| `#2979` | Orphan `Description` key, byte-identical to top-level `description` | Redundant copy removed, zero data loss |\\n| `#2984` | Orphan `Description` key, byte-identical to top-level `description` | Redundant copy removed, zero data loss |\\n| `#2985` | Orphan `Description` key had **diverged** from the top-level field (newer content not present elsewhere) | Unique content merged into `description` via `update_item()` before removing the orphan key \u2014 see comment on #2985 for the full account |\\n| `#2972` | Orphan `Notes` key (throwaway scratch-item test content) | Removed \u2014 closed scratch item, content had zero recovery value |\\n| `#2900` | Orphan `RT-ICA-PROBE` key (test/verification residue) | Removed \u2014 closed item, probe content |\\n| `#2903` | Orphan `RECONCILE-PROBE` key (debug test strings) | Removed \u2014 closed item, probe content |\\n\\nRe-scanned the entire local cache (403 items) after cleanup: **zero items with a bare non-canonical section key remain.**\\n\\nEvery affected item was verified individually post-cleanup: `view_item()` against the live GitHub backend shows zero duplicated `##` headings in the resolved body for all nine items.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reopened 2026 08 19 Closure Was Premature Underlying Defect Still Reproduces Post Fix\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nClosed `completed` on 2026-08-18T08:49:47Z. Real fix commits for the cited root cause did land and are present in the currently-running plugin build (verified: installed `dh@jamie-bitflight-skills` v9.0.19, `installPath: .../dh/9.0.19`, commit `6cb65498`, byte-diffed identical against repo source; `git log 6cb65498..HEAD -- plugins/development-harness/` = 0, confirming no drift):\\n\\n- `bb8ee0c0` fix(development-harness): unify section-key normalization between local writes and GitHub-parsed sections (#2964)\\n- `e9c1d72d` feat(development-harness): canonical section-name registry with write-boundary validation (#2987)\\n- `e4f4a8ba` fix(development-harness): view_item YAML-fallback shows display titles, not raw section keys (#2971)\\n- `c527427e` fix(development-harness): remaining PR #2987 Copilot review findings (#3015)\\n\\n`backlog_core/section_registry.py` now exists with a comprehensive canonical `SectionKey` enum covering RT-ICA, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, and even Working Register (258 lines) \u2014 this is genuine, substantive progress and directly addresses the \\\"3-entry map\\\" gap this issue originally diagnosed in `_normalize_section_key()`.\\n\\n**A fresh live reproduction (2026-08-19, same items #2953 and #2955, post-`/reload-plugins`) shows the user-visible symptom \u2014 `backlog_groom` reports success but content is unreadable via `backlog_view` and never reaches the live GitHub issue \u2014 still occurs, in a mutated form:**\\n\\n- Canonical Title-Case keys DO now get created correctly for most sections (`Dependencies`, `Files`, `Impact`, `Impact Radius`, etc.) \u2014 this part is fixed.\\n- But a SECOND, duplicate lowercase-snake key for the *same content* also gets written alongside it (`Dependencies` + `dependencies`, `Impact Radius` + `impact_radius`, etc.) in the local cache \u2014 29 top-level keys on #2953 where ~11 should exist.\\n- `RT-ICA`, `Fact-Check`, and `Issue Classification` still exist *only* as lowercase-snake (`rt_ica`, `fact_check`, `issue_classification`) with **no** canonical Title-Case/hyphenated key at all, despite `section_registry.py` defining exactly these as canonical `SectionKey` members with Title-Case/hyphenated display strings.\\n- Regardless of key correctness, `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#2955\\\", section=\\\"Working Register\\\")` \u2014 querying the exact canonical name defined in `section_registry.py` line 123 \u2014 returns `{\\\"error\\\":\\\"Section not found: 'Working Register'\\\", \\\"valid_sections\\\":[...only original item-creation sections...]}` immediately after a write that reported `\\\"groomed_updated\\\": true`. Tested fresh, isolated, with a uniquely-marked test string, specifically to rule out stale-cache artifacts.\\n- `gh api repos/Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills/issues/2953` and `.../2955` \u2014 the live issues \u2014 still show zero trace of any of this session's ~20+ successful-looking writes, before this manual edit.\\n\\n**Reproduction steps (exact, repeatable):**\\n\\n1. Have any open GitHub-backend backlog item, e.g. `#2955`.\\n2. Call `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(selector=\\\"#2955\\\", section=\\\"Working Register\\\", append=True, content=\\\"<any unique marker string>\\\")`. Observe response: `\\\"groomed_updated\\\": true`, with a `\\\"Reconciled #2955: 1 provider patch(es), N pending mutation(s), 0 failure(s).\\\"` message.\\n3. Immediately call `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view(selector=\\\"#2955\\\", summary=False, section=\\\"Working Register\\\")`.\\n4. Observe: `{\\\"error\\\":\\\"Section not found: 'Working Register'\\\", \\\"valid_sections\\\": [...only the item's original creation-time sections...]}` \u2014 despite `Working Register` being a defined canonical `SectionKey` in `section_registry.py`.\\n5. Cross-check ground truth: `gh api repos/Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills/issues/2955 --jq '.body'` \u2014 the marker string from step 2 is absent.\\n\\n**Conclusion**: the write-side normalization fix (#2964/#2987) is real and appears to have closed the *write* half of the original diagnosis, but the *read/render* path used by `backlog_view` (and whatever process is supposed to push local cache mutations to the live GitHub issue body) still does not surface newly-written sections at all \u2014 canonical key or not. This looks like a distinct, still-open defect downstream of the write fix, not the same bug recurring identically. `#2974`'s own reproduction (scratch item #2975, same date) found the identical \\\"`section_filter_miss: false` but content absent\\\" signature for the `sections=[...]` filtered-read call shape; this session confirms the same absence for the general `summary=true`/`summary=false` unfiltered read and for exact single-`section=` lookups \u2014 the read-path gap looks total, not shape-specific.\\n\\nRecommend auditing the actual read/render code path `backlog_view` uses to build `sections_index`/`body` for GitHub-backend items \u2014 confirm whether it reads the local YAML cache's `sections` dict at all, or only ever renders from the last-synced GitHub issue body \u2014 and separately audit why local\u2192GitHub push (the \\\"pending mutations\\\" queue, observed at 67+ and not visibly draining across many writes in this session) isn't delivering writes to the live issue.\\n\\nRelated: #2974 (P0 audit of silent-miss consumer impact, still open), #2970 (canonical registry \u2014 now substantially implemented via `section_registry.py`), #2573/#2504 (sync/cache architecture redesign \u2014 may be the right place to fix the push/replay half of this).\\n\\nNote: this update was written directly via `gh api` PATCH to the raw issue body, bypassing `backlog_groom`/`backlog_core`'s section-write path entirely \u2014 deliberately, because that path is the one under test and a comment-only note was confirmed not to be visible to any backlog-consuming process.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fixed 2026 08 20 Root Causes A And B Greenlit By User\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRoot causes A and B (from the diagnosis above) are fixed on branch `issue-2956-backlog-groom-visibility-trace`, commits `3abaf37f` and `c490ca12`. Root cause C (pending-mutation title-mismatch, ~58 legacy items) was explicitly scoped out \u2014 confirmed non-blocking for correctness, tracked as a separate follow-up rather than pulled into this fix.\\n\\n### `3abaf37f` \u2014 Root cause A: `backlog_view` now reads the authoritative body\\n\\n`gh_client.view_enrich_from_github` gained an optional `resolve_version` callback. `GitHubBackend.view_enrich_from_github` now wires it to `self._work_items.work_item_version()` \u2014 the same resolution `_GitHubWorkItemSync.provider_item_from_issue` already used elsewhere in this codebase \u2014 instead of the raw `gh_issue[\\\"body\\\"]`. A resolution failure (content store unavailable, conflicting/malformed head or comment) falls back to the raw issue body rather than failing the view outright.\\n\\n### `c490ca12` \u2014 Root cause B: stale non-canonical section keys now self-heal at load time\\n\\n`rendering.normalize_unknown_sections` redefined \\\"canonical\\\" as \\\"already a registered `SectionKey` value\\\" (checked against `SECTION_HEADING`) instead of \\\"does not start with `unknown__`\\\", so a bare stale key like `Working Register` (pre-`bb8ee0c0` legacy data) now folds into its canonical `working_register` counterpart the same way an `unknown__`-prefixed key already did. Unresolvable keys (`Description`, `Notes`, the `*-PROBE` keys) are left unchanged, as before \u2014 they need a separate migration decision, not a registry fix.\\n\\nAlso fixed in the same commit: `_GitHubWorkItemSync.load_records()` merges cached snapshots (normalized via `yaml_io.load_item`) with queued pending mutations (loaded via plain `BacklogItem.model_validate`, which never normalized) \u2014 and a pending mutation takes precedence over its snapshot for the same reference. An item with a stuck pending mutation (the root-cause-C class) would have kept serving un-normalized sections indefinitely regardless of the registry fix above, since the healed snapshot would never be the one actually returned. Pending-mutation items are now normalized the same way before merging.\\n\\n### Validation\\n\\nRan the exact reproduction steps from this issue's body against live source (direct import, not the installed plugin cache) and the live GitHub backend, on `#2955` \u2014 the item that originally exhibited both the stuck-mutation and stale-key conditions:\\n\\n1. `groom_item(selector=\\\"#2955\\\", section=\\\"Working Register\\\", append=True, content=\\\"<marker>\\\")` \u2192 `groomed_updated: true`, `\\\"1 provider patch(es)\\\"` applied.\\n2. `view_item(selector=\\\"#2955\\\", section=\\\"Working Register\\\")` immediately after \u2192 `section_filter_miss: False` (previously `True`, \\\"Section not found\\\"), marker present in the returned body.\\n3. Resolved body's `## Working Register` heading count: 1 (previously 2 \u2014 the stale bare key's duplicate render is gone).\\n4. Cross-checked directly against the live GitHub issue comment (`gh api .../issues/2955/comments`) \u2014 marker present.\\n\\n`uv run ruff check`, `uv run ruff format --check`, and `uv run ty check` all pass on every touched file. Full `pytest` run: 1079 passed, 9 skipped, no regressions. Diagnostic test-residue entries from both the trace and validation passes were struck from `#2955` and `#929` after use.\\n\\nNot in scope for this fix (tracked separately): root cause C's ~58 legacy items stuck behind the title-mismatch pending-mutation guard, and the unrecoverable `Description`/`Notes`/probe-key class on `#2979`/`#2984`/`#2985`/`#2900`/`#2903` (orphan data that needs a migration decision, not a registry entry).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Merged 2026 08 19\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPR #3040 merged to `main` at `6214055a`. Root causes A and B fixed, the `Description`-write guard added, full blast-radius cleanup done and verified (see above). Closing.\\n\\n**Not in scope here, tracked separately:**\\n- Root cause C \u2014 the ~58 legacy items stuck behind the `#2963` title-mismatch pending-mutation guard. Confirmed non-blocking for correctness this session; still a real gap worth its own fix.\\n- A follow-up lint cleanup (pre-existing unused-argument findings in two test files, unrelated to this bug, found while addressing PR review feedback) is in progress as its own separate PR.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root Cause Diagnosed 2026 08 19 Fresh Session Independent Traces\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRe-reproduced the exact repro steps above against `#2955` this session (marker `REPRO-2956-TRACER-20260819-a91f7c`, since struck). Confirmed the symptom is still real, then dispatched two independent, falsification-tested traces (`dh:codebase-analyzer`, live source, live GitHub backend) \u2014 one for the write path, one for the read/push path. Full reports with confirmation/falsification tests for every claim:\\n\\n- Write path: `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260819-issue-2956-write-path-trace.md`\\n- Read/push path: `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260819-issue-2956-read-path-trace.md`\\n\\n**Headline: this issue's headline symptom has one dominant, universal root cause (A) plus one item-specific aggravating root cause (B). The write-normalization fix (#2964/#2987) is confirmed correct going forward \u2014 it is not the live defect.**\\n\\n### Root cause A (dominant) \u2014 `backlog_view` reads the wrong document for every GitHub-backend item\\n\\nGitHub has two body representations for a work item in this system's design: the raw human-owned `issue.body`, and the agent-owned authoritative body reachable via a `dh-content` head record \u2192 tagged audit comment (see `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \\\"GitHub writable records\\\"). `backlog_groom` writes correctly land in the **second** representation \u2014 confirmed: the write posts a new issue comment via `render_work_item_comment` and CAS-writes a `WorkItemHead` record; `gh api .../issues/2955/comments` shows the comment landing at the exact write timestamp, marker included, heading `## Working Register` present.\\n\\n`backlog_view`, however, unconditionally reads the **first**: `gh_client.view_enrich_from_github` does `result.body = gh_issue[\\\"body\\\"]` \u2014 the raw GraphQL issue body \u2014 with no branch that resolves the head/comment representation `backlog_groom` actually writes to. `operations._assemble_view_content` then derives `valid_sections`/`section_filter_miss` from `## `/`### ` headings of that raw body via regex. Confirmed character-for-character: `gh api .../issues/2955 --jq '.body' | grep -E '^#{2,3} '` produces exactly the `valid_sections` array `backlog_view` returned.\\n\\nFalsified independently: running the same call against live source with `GITHUB_TOKEN` unset (raw-body enrichment unavailable) correctly falls through to `item.sections` and finds `Working Register`. With the token set (normal operation), it does not. Same code, same item, same section name \u2014 only the presence of the raw-body overwrite differs.\\n\\nThe reconciliation path in this same codebase already resolves this correctly (`_GitHubWorkItemSync.provider_item_from_issue` calls `work_item_version()` and uses `version.body`) \u2014 only the view path's GitHub enrichment bypasses it. This affects every GitHub-backend item uniformly, independent of any section-key naming issue.\\n\\n**This is why the write-normalization fix did not resolve the symptom**: the fix corrected which key a write lands under, but `backlog_view` was never reading that structure to begin with.\\n\\n### Root cause B (aggravating, item-specific) \u2014 pre-registry stale keys never heal, causing real data loss on GitHub-body reparse\\n\\n`#2955`'s local cache carries a stale `Working Register` (Title-Case, bare) key alongside the canonical `working_register` key. This was **not** created by the current write path \u2014 reproduced live on a clean item (`#929`, four fresh section writes including `Working Register`, `RT-ICA`, `Fact-Check`, `Issue Classification`): all four landed under exactly one canonical snake_case key each, zero duplication, zero `unknown__` keys. The stale `Working Register` key on `#2955` predates commit `bb8ee0c0` (`git log -S'\\\"Working Register\\\"'` confirms the name didn't exist in the registry before that commit; the stale key's entries are all timestamped before it) \u2014 it's legacy data from the pre-registry code path that returned an unresolved name verbatim.\\n\\nThe live defect: `rendering.normalize_unknown_sections` (the only load-time healing fold) only heals keys with an `unknown__` prefix \u2014 a bare non-canonical key like `Working Register` is treated as already-canonical and never folded into `working_register`, even though `resolve_section_name(\\\"Working Register\\\")` resolves cleanly. `github_sync.merge_item`'s `set(local) | set(remote)` union means nothing else ever drops it either \u2014 it's permanent.\\n\\nThis is not cosmetic. `github_sync.render_issue_body` emits `## Working Register` twice (once for the canonical key via the entry-bearing loop, once for the bare Title-Case key via the unknown-sections loop, since `_KNOWN_SECTION_KEYS` is snake-key-only). `parsing.extract_sections` is a dict comprehension keyed on heading text \u2014 last occurrence wins. Confirmed by round-tripping `#2955`'s real cache item through the real functions: canonical section carrying 9 entries (including every recent write) renders first and is **silently discarded on reparse**, leaving only the stale 7-entry version. Same mechanism as this issue's own `#2955` Working Register entry from 2026-08-18: \\\"`backlog_groom` writes report success but are invisible to every read path.\\\"\\n\\nBlast radius (scanned all cached items): `#2953` carries 13 such recoverable bare keys (worst-affected item, matches this issue's original observation). `#2979`/`#2984`/`#2985` carry an **unrecoverable** `Description` section key \u2014 `parse_issue_body` special-cases the `## Description` heading as `item.description`, so a `Description` section key can never round-trip regardless of any registry fix; it's write-only orphan data.\\n\\nThe RT-ICA/Fact-Check/Issue-Classification \\\"snake-only, no Title-Case counterpart\\\" observation from the 2026-08-19 reproduction above is **not** a behavioral divergence \u2014 reproduced identically on `#929`: those three land snake-only today, same as `Working Register` does on a clean item. The asymmetry on `#2955`/`#2953` is registration timing (those three were the *only* three registered pre-`bb8ee0c0`, so no other name has ever had a chance to accumulate the same kind of stale sibling), not a live code-path difference.\\n\\n### Root cause C (pre-existing, separate class) \u2014 pending mutations don't drain\\n\\nThe \\\"pending mutation(s)\\\" counter (66, reproduced this session; previously observed at 29 and 67+ in other sessions) sums two durable queues in `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/github-cache/cache.yaml`. For `#2955`, the queued work-item mutation is permanently stuck because `reconcile()`'s acknowledge predicate requires an exact title match between the local record and the live GitHub title, and the live title carries a `chore: ` prefix (added by `gh_client.create_issue_for_item`) that was never persisted back to the local record \u2014 this is the already-identified `#2963` class, affecting roughly 58 legacy items created before that guard existed. A separate 4-entry leak has empty `metadata.issue`. A third factor: `_reconcile_groomed_item` always uses `ReconcileScope.TARGETED` on the single item being groomed, so no groom call ever sweeps any other item's stuck mutation. **The queue count is a health-signal false alarm, not evidence that any given write failed** \u2014 every write reproduced this session landed correctly in its comment/head representation regardless of queue depth.\\n\\n### Corrected understanding vs. this issue's earlier text\\n\\n- \\\"local\u2192GitHub push... isn't delivering writes to the live issue\\\" (line above, 2026-08-19 update) \u2014 **incorrect as stated**. Writes reach GitHub every time, in their designed representation (audit comment + head record), confirmed via `gh api .../comments`. The push/replay mechanism is not broken; `backlog_view` simply never looks at what it delivered.\\n- \\\"write-normalization fix... appears to have closed the *write* half\\\" \u2014 **confirmed true for the write path itself** (see root cause B's `#929` reproduction: zero duplication on fresh writes). The `Working Register`/`working_register` duplication visible on `#2955` today is 100% pre-existing stale data from before the fix, not new duplication \u2014 it looked like a recurrence only because nothing ever healed it.\\n\\n### Fix directions (diagnosis only \u2014 nothing implemented this session)\\n\\n1. **Root cause A** (highest priority \u2014 universal, blocks every consumer of `backlog_view` on every GitHub-backend item): route `gh_client.view_enrich_from_github` through the same head/comment resolution `_GitHubWorkItemSync.provider_item_from_issue` already uses, instead of the raw `gh_issue[\\\"body\\\"]`.\\n2. **Root cause B**: widen `normalize_unknown_sections`'s fold gate from \\\"key starts with `unknown__`\\\" to \\\"key is not a registered canonical `SectionKey` value\\\" \u2014 this requires redefining `is_canonical_key` consistently with the existing `canonical_owns` tie-break logic per the `#3015` Copilot review note already in that function's docstring. Separately decide a policy for the unrecoverable `Description`/`Notes`/probe-key class (`#2979`/`#2984`/`#2985`/`#2900`/`#2903`) \u2014 likely a one-time migration rather than a registry entry, since `Description` can never be a section per `parse_issue_body`'s existing special-case.\\n3. **Root cause C**: persist the GitHub-assigned title (including any `type: ` prefix) back to the local record at issue-creation time so the title-equality guard can pass for future items; separately decide whether to write a migration/backfill for the ~58 already-stuck legacy mutations, or accept them as permanently unacknowledgeable given the queue count is not otherwise harmful to correctness.\\n\\nRelated: #2974 (P0 audit of silent-miss consumer impact \u2014 root cause A directly explains its `section_filter_miss: false but content absent` signature too, since that's the same raw-body vs. authoritative-body gap manifesting through a different `backlog_view` call shape), #2970 (canonical registry \u2014 root cause B confirms it's complete for the *write* path; the *read-time healing* gap is the remaining piece), #2963 (title-mismatch class, root cause C).\\n\\nTest residue from this session's reproduction (marker entries on `#2955` and `#929`) has been struck via `backlog_strike_entry`.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"cb5858d2ac2216b4f151db5379fd327e020b5c84c2a3a603e5b1ec853de02b7d\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRgUcSQ\"}"}